From: Florian Fuchs <fuchsfl@gmail.com>
Date: Fri, 30 Oct 2026 21:00:00 +0100
Subject: [PATCH] sh: maple: Wake waiters whenever a device goes idle

Drivers like vmu-flash and vmu-lcd wait on maple_wait for the busy flag
of their device to clear before they queue a packet, with a timeout of
one second. The maple core clears busy for the replies to its own
packets too, but only wakes the waiters for DATATRF, FILEERR and OK.

The periodic DEVINFO poll marks the device busy every MAPLE_PNP_INTERVAL,
which is also one second. A driver that runs into it sleeps through its
whole timeout and then usually finds the next poll in flight, failing
with "VMU at (2, 1) is busy" and an I/O error on a read of the
filesystem.

Wake the waiters on every path that clears busy.

---
diff --git a/drivers/sh/maple/maple.c b/drivers/sh/maple/maple.c
--- a/drivers/sh/maple/maple.c
+++ b/drivers/sh/maple/maple.c
@@ -581,6 +581,7 @@
 	}
 	/* Some hardware devices generate false detach messages on unit 0 */
 	atomic_set(&mdev->busy, 0);
+	wake_up(&mdev->maple_wait);
 }
 
 /* preprocess hotplugs or scans */
@@ -676,6 +677,14 @@
 			case MAPLE_RESPONSE_DEVINFO:
 				maple_response_devinfo(mdev, recvbuf);
 				atomic_set(&mdev->busy, 0);
+				/*
+				* The periodic DEVINFO poll marks the device
+				* busy too; wake drivers that wait to queue
+				* their own packet, or they sleep until their
+				* timeout and, as the poll repeats every
+				* MAPLE_PNP_INTERVAL, find it busy again.
+				*/
+				wake_up(&mdev->maple_wait);
 				break;
 
 			case MAPLE_RESPONSE_DATATRF:
@@ -698,6 +707,7 @@
 					" 0x%X at (%d, %d)\n", code,
 					mdev->port, mdev->unit);
 				atomic_set(&mdev->busy, 0);
+				wake_up(&mdev->maple_wait);
 				break;
 
 			case MAPLE_RESPONSE_ALLINFO:
@@ -706,6 +716,7 @@
 				" but call is not supported\n", mdev->port,
 				mdev->unit);
 				atomic_set(&mdev->busy, 0);
+				wake_up(&mdev->maple_wait);
 				break;
 
 			case MAPLE_RESPONSE_OK:
--
2.43.0
